WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.275
Picture a January morning on Tampa Bay.

2
00:00:03.525 --> 00:00:08.250
You leave the dock in sunshine, and half a mile out, the world turns white.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.650
You can’t see your own bow rail.

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.100
Which steering rules apply now?

5
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.400
Not the ones we’ve been studying.

6
00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.350
This is Rule nineteen, and it’s one of the most tested rules in the book.

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.100
It applies to vessels not in sight of one another.

8
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:30.925
And it applies when you’re navigating in or near an area of restricted visibility.

9
00:00:31.175 --> 00:00:33.300
Notice the words or near.

10
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:42.025
You can be in clear air, running alongside a fog bank, and Rule nineteen still applies to you and to whatever is inside that bank.

11
00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.125
Now the big idea.

12
00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:49.250
Under Rule nineteen, there is no stand-on vessel and no give-way vessel.

13
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.825
None.

14
00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:55.525
The crossing rule, the head-on rule and the ladder are all switched off.

15
00:00:55.775 --> 00:01:01.375
Instead, every vessel shares the same duty to avoid a close-quarters situation.

16
00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:05.850
Nobody gets to hold her course and wait for the other boat to act.
